1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,433
Welcome to Class 6: protected project websites.

2
00:00:03,433 --> 00:00:07,847
This video introduces the core decisions and working patterns.

3
00:00:07,847 --> 00:00:14,714
Watch the complete lesson first, then use the written class page for copyable commands,

4
00:00:14,714 --> 00:00:17,166
exercises, and detailed reference material.

5
00:00:17,166 --> 00:00:23,650
By the end of this class, you should be able to: decide whether a project idea

6
00:00:23,650 --> 00:00:30,133
belongs on the governed vhost platform; distinguish a static information site from an active project application;

7
00:00:30,133 --> 00:00:36,617
explain what RCC provides and what the project application must provide; use the copyable example without

8
00:00:36,617 --> 00:00:43,101
creating local accounts or exposing storage paths; prepare a complete vhost request for project-lead and administrator

9
00:00:43,101 --> 00:00:48,369
review; recognize when computation belongs in Slurm rather than in a web request.

10
00:00:48,369 --> 00:00:53,780
Good candidates include: project documentation and study information; read-only dashboards and reports;

11
00:00:53,780 --> 00:00:59,643
search or query interfaces over an approved internal database; curated collections of reports,

12
00:00:59,643 --> 00:01:05,506
images or result files; small project tools used by an identified project group;

13
00:01:05,506 --> 00:01:10,466
bounded forms or upload workflows with a defined destination and owner.

14
00:01:10,466 --> 00:01:17,682
A vhost is particularly useful when medical professionals or biomedical researchers need a browser-based interface and

15
00:01:17,682 --> 00:01:21,290
should not be expected to install command-line tools.

16
00:01:21,290 --> 00:01:28,696
Inside the hospital network, an active project application can use the low-friction RCC username/password or passkey

17
00:01:28,696 --> 00:01:29,159
flow.

18
00:01:29,159 --> 00:01:32,862
Outside the hospital, the gateway requires stronger authentication.

19
00:01:32,862 --> 00:01:40,267
Informational static sites may be available without login inside the hospital, but active applications always require

20
00:01:40,267 --> 00:01:41,656
an individual identity.

21
00:01:41,656 --> 00:01:46,285
The backend application is not directly reachable by ordinary users.

22
00:01:46,285 --> 00:01:53,691
This is important: the trusted identity information is meaningful only when it arrives through the RCC

23
00:01:53,691 --> 00:01:54,154
gateway.

24
00:01:54,154 --> 00:02:01,024
Junior developers are not expected to administer the authentication gateway, integrity checker or detection systems.

25
00:02:01,024 --> 00:02:06,979
They are expected to follow the application contract and keep their software maintainable.

26
00:02:06,979 --> 00:02:13,408
### Database-backed interface Prefer a dedicated application account with access to approved views or tables.

27
00:02:13,408 --> 00:02:17,695
A read-only dashboard should not receive write or administration privileges.

28
00:02:17,695 --> 00:02:24,553
### Curated file collection Store a catalogue that maps an opaque identifier to an approved file.

29
00:02:24,553 --> 00:02:28,411
The browser supplies the identifier, not a filesystem path.

30
00:02:28,411 --> 00:02:32,697
The application checks project membership again before returning the file.

31
00:02:32,697 --> 00:02:38,269
### Computational result portal The website may collect bounded parameters and display results,

32
00:02:38,269 --> 00:02:42,984
but substantial processing should be handled asynchronously through an approved workflow.

33
00:02:42,984 --> 00:02:48,557
Do not keep a browser request open while a large scientific analysis runs.

34
00:02:48,557 --> 00:02:51,986
### Upload workflow Use a staging directory, server-generated.

35
00:02:51,986 --> 00:02:58,290
A project lead describes the purpose, users, data type and required application pattern.

36
00:02:58,290 --> 00:03:03,624
RCC Admin selects the fixed security recipe that matches the function.

37
00:03:03,624 --> 00:03:08,473
A different administrator reviews the request and its data boundaries.

38
00:03:08,473 --> 00:03:11,867
An operator reviews the generated Git change.

39
00:03:11,867 --> 00:03:15,746
Deployment, local health checks and access tests run.

40
00:03:15,746 --> 00:03:22,050
The project owner periodically confirms that the site is still needed and maintained.

41
00:03:22,050 --> 00:03:26,414
Developers describe required functions, upstream service and data dependencies.

42
00:03:26,414 --> 00:03:32,233
They cannot disable authentication, integrity monitoring, logging, request limits or gateway-only access.

43
00:03:32,233 --> 00:03:38,687
The example demonstrates: trusted identity handling; project-group checks; a read-only database query;

44
00:03:38,687 --> 00:03:45,678
curated downloads using opaque identifiers; safe failure for direct clients and invalid requests.

45
00:03:45,678 --> 00:03:50,519
Local demonstration mode accepts synthetic headers only from loopback.

46
00:03:50,519 --> 00:03:55,897
Production must use the governed RCC gateway and deployment workflow.

47
00:03:55,897 --> 00:04:02,508
You have completed this class when: all example unit tests pass; you can explain which of

48
00:04:02,508 --> 00:04:09,119
the four site patterns fits your project; the app refuses direct clients and missing project membership;

49
00:04:09,119 --> 00:04:14,904
no filesystem path is accepted from the URL; the request template names an owner,

50
00:04:14,904 --> 00:04:21,516
project, data classification, application pattern, data dependency and review date; you can identify any heavy computation

51
00:04:21,516 --> 00:04:23,582
that must move to Slurm.

